WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.090
(indistinct chatter)
(soft music)

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.470
- [Reporter] These videos show the moment

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.473
thousands of rioters charged
through Brazil's Congress,

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:12.210
presidential Palace and Supreme Court.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.820
Supporters of former
President Jair Bolsonaro

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.032
smashed windows and ripped up documents.

00:00:17.032 --> 00:00:20.250
The scenes reminiscent
of the January 6th ride

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.290
on the US Capitol came amid protest

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.030
over Brazil's election results

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.680
and days after the inauguration

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.380
of Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.460
In the lead up to the vote,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.550
Bolsonaro alleged election
fraud without evidence

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.792
and he left the country
after the election.

00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:37.230
So as tensions rise between supporters

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.044
of the two main political
factions in Brazil,

00:00:40.044 --> 00:00:41.280
(indistinct chatter)

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.620
here are the key moments in
the months leading to the siege

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.773
that can help us understand
how and why the chaos unfolded.

00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:50.850
First, let's rewind to late October.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.850
That's when leftist, Luiz
Inácio Lula da Silva,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.043
won the presidential election runoff.

00:00:56.043 --> 00:00:58.920
The vote was the closest
in the country's history

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.920
with Da Silva taking 50.9% the
vote to 49.1% for Bolsonaro.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.547
It was also a highly polarizing campaign.

00:01:07.547 --> 00:01:11.214
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.040
With voters on the left and on the right,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.740
divided over issues
including economic insecurity

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.307
and Brazil's handling of the pandemic.

00:01:19.307 --> 00:01:22.974
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.930
Following the loss, Bolsonaro
supporters show their anger

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.820
by camping outside military barracks

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.036
calling for the army to
remove the president-elect.

00:01:33.036 --> 00:01:36.060
(crowd protesting)

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.010
In his first remarks following his defeat,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.540
Bolsonaro thanked his supporters

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.320
and said he'd followed the country's rules

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.506
but he didn't concede and he
didn't congratulate Da Silva.

00:01:44.506 --> 00:01:48.173
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:02.964 --> 00:02:05.310
And the same day protests
from his supporters continued,

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.380
truck drivers blocked
roads across the country

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.390
rejecting Bolsonaro's loss.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.070
The crowds only began to disperse

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.410
after Bolsonaro's government
confirmed the transition

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.940
toward a new administration

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.764
and asked supporters
to stop blocking roads.

00:02:17.764 --> 00:02:21.431
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.684
But more unrest followed.

00:02:28.684 --> 00:02:31.410
When Da Silva's election
victory was certified

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.490
by the federal court,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.290
Bolsonaro supporters held a series

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.937
of violent protests in Brasilia.

00:02:36.937 --> 00:02:41.508
A bus was set on fire, leading to arrest.

00:02:41.508 --> 00:02:45.175
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.588
At the end of December,

00:02:47.588 --> 00:02:48.667
Bolsonaro encouraged his supporters

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.525
"not to throw in the towel."

00:02:50.525 --> 00:02:54.192
(speaking foreign language)

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.610
On the same day, the outgoing
president left his country

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.200
and photos from ROTA show that

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.030
Brazil's equivalent of Air Force one

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.070
landed in Orlando, Florida.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.360
That meant he was out of the country

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.850
for Da Silva's inauguration
ceremony in Brasilia.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.350
And when protestors targeted

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.305
Brazil's most important
government buildings.

00:03:16.305 --> 00:03:18.805
(guns firing)

00:03:20.431 --> 00:03:22.860
Police in Brazil detained
more than 1500 people

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.840
accused of taking part in the unrest.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.220
And the Supreme Court removed

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.050
Brazilian's Federal District Governor,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.540
a Bolsonaro ally from his post.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.850
After the siege, Bolsonaro
condemned the acts

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.400
saying that any vandalism or
invasion of public buildings

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.930
was unacceptable.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.960
A day later, he was briefly hospitalized

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.300
after complaining of stomach
pains related to injuries

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.120
he suffered years prior
during a near fatal stabbing.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.646
That's according to his wife.

00:03:46.646 --> 00:03:49.230
Da Silva visited Brasilia after the riots

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.572
and his supporters also
took to the streets.

00:03:51.572 --> 00:03:54.360
The current president's
also divisive figure

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.160
due to his role in a corruption scandal

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.680
as well as for the socialist
policies he put in place

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.300
during his first mandate.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.566
He has promised to investigate
how the riots unfolded.

00:04:03.566 --> 00:04:07.316
(speaking foreign language)

